<!--
  Reduced from the live national NAAD document named in issue #45
  (urn:oid:2.49.0.1.124.3426017992.2026): four <info> blocks, one per
  (language × area group) — en/fr "active" expiring +16 h, en/fr "ended"
  expiring +1 h. The 29-entry <references> list is trimmed to two and each
  polygon to a triangle; the <parameter> blocks are verbatim, including the
  duplicated 1.0/1.1 Alert_Location_Status layers.

  Geography: both groups are Alberta (SGC 48…), so province mode matches both
  and must prefer the active one. The rings are disjoint — the active group
  sits around 52.0/-114.0, the ended group around 50.0/-112.0 — so a GPS point
  can select one group without touching the other.

  Timestamps are pinned to 2099 rather than the captured 2026 dates so the
  clock never reaches them: these tests are about the Alert_Location_Status
  lifecycle rule, and a wall-clock expiry would mark every block terminal and
  hide whichever defect the test is pinning. The +16 h / +1 h spread between
  the two groups — the shape that lets an ended alert linger — is preserved.
-->
